[{:seon.task/name "the-unit-tap-reports-every-macro-expanded"
  :seon.task/plan "Shrink brief 3, item 16 (worktree session SHRINK2, 2026-10-23, read on main 8a5818c): seon.program/macro-uses re-reads each form's text and resolves every list head in its namespace to find the macros it calls, because the analyzed node is the expansion and names no macro. That reading can disagree with what the compiler did: a local named like a macro, a quoted list, a head shadowed by a binding (no reading of code where the compiler's facts exist: docs/source.md#a-forms-account-is-the-programs-facts). Cause, in Jolt: jolt-core/jolt/analyzer.clj expands a macro call knowing its var (macro-symbol ctx head) and records it only in *expansion-box* (:120), a diagnostic box holding the innermost expansion, reset as analysis returns; nothing reports it to the unit tap (:121-, jolt.analyzer/*unit-tap*), so the tap's consumer (seon.program/tap) cannot know which macros a unit expanded. Correct, on a jolt/<topic> branch of our Jolt: at that expansion site, when a unit is being tapped, add the macro's qualified symbol to the unit's set of expanded macros (every expansion, nested ones included), and hand that set to the unit tap with the form and node (a third argument, or a key on the node's unit info). Then seon.program/tap records it in the account (:macros), seon.program/dependents reads it, and macro-uses and its re-read go. Proofs: a Jolt test that a unit expanding a macro which expands another reports both; seon.program-test: a form whose local shadows a macro's name records no macro use (red today through macro-uses), and dependents of a changed macro are unchanged otherwise."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.analyzer-test/unit-expanding-macro-expands-another-reports-both-qualified}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-defmacro-account-holds-its-doc-and-arglists"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: deps. Extends jolt.analyzer's def account (the unit tap, *unit-tap*), which seon.program/account reads. Measured at root [tests-4]: (seon.source/accounts 'app.x \"(defmacro m \\\"the macro's doc\\\" [x] x)\") answers a def whose :meta is #{:macro} only: no :doc, no :arglists. A defn's account holds both. So an analysis without running (another tree's file, main's forms imported by a root on older code) gets a macro's docstring and arglists only from the live var. seon.program/account reads the live var for a macro for that reason alone (the macro clause beside its ran? rule); with the analyzer recording them, that clause goes, and a macro is analyzed from its text as every other def is. Cost: none added, the expansion already holds the doc string and params. Proof: seon.program-test/a-macro-analyzed-without-running-keeps-its-doc-and-arglists stays green with the macro clause deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.program-test/a-macro-analyzed-without-running-keeps-its-doc-and-arglists}
  :seon.task/needs #{}}]
